#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d923a3-db7f-532b-a813-4f0f8279c875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326488} {:answer/id #uuid "5918cfd2-1259-5fc3-8344-c3492582a735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326489} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f6c20b-558c-5896-a131-57fa545f27c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326490} {:answer/id #uuid "75954da1-6d61-56d1-90af-589130ec7483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326491} {:answer/id #uuid "1b696a28-6a1d-5183-b89c-7be33107d7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75954da1-6d61-56d1-90af-589130ec7483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a136a672-4f55-5841-8df6-a4fe0d07dfcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "823545bb-0540-59df-9354-6b5f7bce2ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-101506" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(1,5,-1)$, $Q(5,1,1)$, and $R(2,5,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046481/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd9b67a-76ed-56b8-bb19-8e8966dd7b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288245} {:answer/id #uuid "638f3270-f2fa-53d1-b4f2-4561b59da7b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288246} {:answer/id #uuid "05e21986-1ecc-56d8-9054-3274d697b44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288247} {:answer/id #uuid "2790e3f5-7ccb-5774-9136-e3aad5ed0f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288248} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4c79e1-f6cb-58d1-8be6-e60bddeb1606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2790e3f5-7ccb-5774-9136-e3aad5ed0f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288248} :answer-field/id #uuid "5022f738-e898-595d-b424-20d982092914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11791463-1fc2-5c5f-8fd7-000137541322" :question/math-academy-id "q-10567" :question/problem "$\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =3x-2\\pi ,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ) & ={csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (7+4{x}^{3}+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7+7{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7[1+{cot}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi )])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+7{csc}^{2}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+7(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi ))+C \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-2\\pi )+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "699a2e70-8eaa-5123-b840-75433e713f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269632} {:answer/id #uuid "c90f12c5-08c6-5467-84c7-cba2de905019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269633} {:answer/id #uuid "80e49bc2-ebd8-592b-8f80-190873bae453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269634} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1aef5f-d9b3-5cd5-b54a-9fa7143e59c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269635} {:answer/id #uuid "6474dc8b-100b-584c-8143-5ad2a59f18ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6474dc8b-100b-584c-8143-5ad2a59f18ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269636} :answer-field/id #uuid "060b8a99-9954-5d74-b747-2b4de118f6c0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c205fa0-d12d-510f-bf38-5376ffd48764" :question/math-academy-id "q-107499" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d3a88c-7ad8-57fd-9c72-7b52d059228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369023} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bb1365-7088-5b65-86dd-73e3bcd87292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369024} {:answer/id #uuid "10e4f492-372c-569e-ab2a-68c9e0bb25d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369025} {:answer/id #uuid "fa84e3a5-6f80-5b1a-8f2b-de06bf6d89be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369026} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad8773-dfb7-510f-b62a-c1f0ba7a323a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e4f492-372c-569e-ab2a-68c9e0bb25d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369025} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c7f961-8bd0-5161-8904-d148e0c79084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac7001d-ff73-507a-96fa-7c0e89246538" :question/math-academy-id "q-109090" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18abaef5-2dc8-5961-bd2a-e6cc52cce7bb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Squared Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b8edcc-96fd-5f4f-b5d1-f14e6777bf16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369030} {:answer/id #uuid "208d15e2-290f-5d19-90a7-cac6761f6e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369031} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b781f1-21d5-516f-a56f-2aba6d4471d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369032} {:answer/id #uuid "91fe8502-88a9-5946-bb20-f9f217bfbc2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369033} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369034} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a580a8-b326-5f5d-842f-75c055c84bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "499118d7-5d2d-512d-85b0-2761f7186709" :question/math-academy-id "q-110220" :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $9$ | $81$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=9)=0.35+0.1=0.45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d7e182-b298-5f94-a314-a10b321bf8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269810} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f44493-de44-5faa-88f8-7da0deb55732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)-2,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "0e152e1c-49fa-5f3f-8b76-4d8b625cbcd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+1,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269812} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} {:answer/id #uuid "117f14a6-a963-5d5c-a450-a45c7d8fe2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(0)+3,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb2a16ed-1194-57ef-bf8e-e232c0235a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{0,2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d7a8c7-a5d5-5b11-a77e-2f92af341958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b3de9cf-84d9-5ffb-a26d-3666f3702b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-110799" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4)=\\operatorname{log}(2x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(4) & =\\operatorname{log}(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}(8) & =\\operatorname{log}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8718fa61-5101-5d42-8ff5-a4623b342288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369037} {:answer/id #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369038} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa2ed43-626c-52f6-bd69-46f64fcc1498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369039} {:answer/id #uuid "b76e551d-542c-5953-b371-3eb6aa5c1290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369040} {:answer/id #uuid "91dc9fea-f836-5738-a361-7ddb57dd111c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369038} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0d6d73-e8ba-5736-8685-c62c1ec3e267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698a78eb-ef71-5b2a-bf3e-b4f41a43c1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111083" :question/problem "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c5a147-0100-5f4b-a221-25b8674b2115" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Integral of a Sum of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06553106-a12c-55fb-812c-e0b0b315f7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(511)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270147} {:answer/id #uuid "86a1de37-572c-5e18-a0da-fb5327fd0535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(529)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270148} {:answer/id #uuid "036dfa90-10a1-5de8-beab-810972593003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(511)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270149} {:answer/id #uuid "c82bb0ca-db63-58f5-a235-e41d3e9c705d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(255)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270150} {:answer/id #uuid "edc8c44c-9813-5bb4-97ea-a07db1c2b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc8c44c-9813-5bb4-97ea-a07db1c2b7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270151} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a45c653-67a6-5187-b484-12b0dbb9a2f4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e33d82b-67b7-5f1d-84c4-6afcc46ec7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114209" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "We split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}1-\\operatorname{arctan}0)+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed7bf75-527f-5614-b21a-8bb91ca67023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294215} {:answer/id #uuid "829abec4-a7a3-56c9-9cce-9d5b74d86141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294216} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eb423-01a3-5a7a-89c8-2c40be7e5979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294217} {:answer/id #uuid "54cfdbe3-cccb-5d5f-95af-07b8a4c9fb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294218} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0143d-3c0c-55f3-a8a9-6591264d34a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eb423-01a3-5a7a-89c8-2c40be7e5979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294217} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3dc11a-e19c-57fc-851b-1872ab159010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e85b0db-5e58-5d04-ba1f-e556cc78eeff" :question/math-academy-id "q-114473" :question/problem "$\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ with $\\theta =4x+3,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3)={csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8+8{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x & =8\\int (1+{cot}^{2}⁡(4x+3))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(4x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)]+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+3)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed1761-ceeb-5033-afa2-cb735e6bb96c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff4a42a-1044-511f-a09b-090c1f1833ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294221} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0cb6f7-7fd4-579a-9bcd-c11391187965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294222} {:answer/id #uuid "301fd970-136e-53c6-98e5-15f739d97651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294223} {:answer/id #uuid "7752c85f-3405-5a76-a9e2-c4f837460361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294224} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3f1780-e729-5653-8d50-90305dcf183e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa0cb6f7-7fd4-579a-9bcd-c11391187965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294222} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0a2e6e-f807-509f-aaad-0b1f33c9e9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" :question/math-academy-id "q-114961" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be8d1c5e-e358-58c6-a688-f0180865145a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294227} {:answer/id #uuid "1abadc92-842b-58eb-a6a7-9ab692f36d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294228} {:answer/id #uuid "b366c2c0-0315-56d9-bb66-b41e7e9793e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294229} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ce34fa-6acb-5ae0-8905-f4e016ed1567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294230} {:answer/id #uuid "26d66dc3-79c7-5ea4-bd7e-c8e05de330f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=3(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ce34fa-6acb-5ae0-8905-f4e016ed1567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294230} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c49152a-535a-5c02-bc12-65fe628a29f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c976869-dd96-5219-84a7-962a7edc295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115511" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956c66aa-326f-5a06-be61-c05003bfa3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369044} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca48a55-b183-5ba6-bab7-3d758f101f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369045} {:answer/id #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369046} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d8fc59-52e9-5ee5-9939-6579d13a3e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369047} {:answer/id #uuid "8800b3d1-7cf8-51e0-944b-60d87f9f0afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369046} :answer-field/id #uuid "2458073f-13ac-5d6c-aef7-398cb69aef1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e1d7d5-e8e8-5e32-9fd2-ba17c7038c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-118552" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62a350c9-d02e-57cd-8aac-1637a092b1fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294233} {:answer/id #uuid "532f4450-bd24-56fc-9836-8c1508ff9172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294234} {:answer/id #uuid "442fc634-b9f7-5e5d-ac0a-5387321dbc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294235} {:answer/id #uuid "65e589fa-49c8-5f91-88d6-354f06544c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294236} {:answer/id #uuid "6fccf9f5-4397-57c4-9c73-690a092a3669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fccf9f5-4397-57c4-9c73-690a092a3669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a9d216-68e8-5934-83cd-fee5c57faee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a918518-91f1-50bb-bef6-5c2c5dd04e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119865" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c55b1c-2629-5f05-89b5-45e391df353d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)+2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270457} {:answer/id #uuid "62e37908-4e24-5ea2-aba3-d760e0d9d0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270458} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2ae01f-f370-503d-9b9e-b324625c9394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270459} {:answer/id #uuid "0b11ab8e-a4d3-5fbd-9394-f2ea808f43bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)+1,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270460} {:answer/id #uuid "49deb945-0e38-5092-8e3f-ed774a8bcc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((11)-2,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e37908-4e24-5ea2-aba3-d760e0d9d0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270458} :answer-field/id #uuid "07e4ea2f-f53a-5af1-9229-d78a463f9c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b65dbd4c-66de-5fa9-bf68-86d78bdb1d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-120300" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,-11).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-11)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the negative half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82dbdc2f-390b-5464-afc5-a6e0401eb8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369051} {:answer/id #uuid "ea11022f-b894-57fc-8592-605dd62043ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369052} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c754b-eeac-5261-b97f-ae67f63753ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369053} {:answer/id #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369054} {:answer/id #uuid "ce84bfcc-1740-5f0a-9e05-b303c55f346b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369054} :answer-field/id #uuid "15aa5794-c6e7-58c5-875f-8b6c68d5310f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4f38637-761c-59db-ada2-8df3d6501eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126031" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe335eb-dc4a-5ea0-83cf-ad929e19b908" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection and Shifting of a Sine or Cosine Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea6a1c0a-b897-5392-8b63-6dcef02fcfb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270782} {:answer/id #uuid "35dbc459-f8e6-5c06-9333-af1766e7c252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270783} {:answer/id #uuid "bc160c85-41a4-53ff-a6b2-6903f45b3946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270784} {:answer/id #uuid "bc10aae8-da97-5c62-a84b-319ab90d723f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270785} {:answer/id #uuid "6007d829-05c8-5678-a6c6-41861cc0c9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\operatorname{cos}x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc160c85-41a4-53ff-a6b2-6903f45b3946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270784} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e558b6a-76b4-5c0f-9a7e-65f7da395130" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af276f5b-4246-5be5-bc99-ae92802b70cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-12669" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e92694d3fc8d6be220fc4d45c0d210a53076db72daad477c3199ece5c4460627.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $3$ units **down** to give $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\operatorname{cos}x-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aecbb8b-bda3-5956-9b0f-98509330a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c19ff0-9677-53d2-8b73-50b898324319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357879} {:answer/id #uuid "6774dedc-fdd4-5f55-be9b-b1aa49760ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s+1),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s+1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357880} {:answer/id #uuid "aeeacb70-038c-551d-b4e6-56d4a72d15d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)]⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357881} {:answer/id #uuid "b691cd9a-7c67-541d-8d68-db83364d3b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357882} {:answer/id #uuid "eba30440-2828-585a-aced-8adc91cef4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eba30440-2828-585a-aced-8adc91cef4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357883} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fe88485-d555-5d33-9b5f-a72c01952ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d60c8dc-149c-57b3-9b22-a7e5b2f8c7d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-127727" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s={e}^{t}-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}-1 & =s \\\\ {e}^{t} & =s+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-(s+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)],\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+1)]⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82dac9ef-b0b4-5b93-94aa-2bebeddc2c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284297} {:answer/id #uuid "542fed80-23be-5a6a-9fdd-47aa3c2e1b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284298} {:answer/id #uuid "3aac59d5-cc4e-57d8-88a4-356b447d2602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284299} {:answer/id #uuid "e224fd99-46a1-5abf-af6d-eebe5f79fbb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284300} {:answer/id #uuid "fc794510-f10f-5ac1-a703-308df3b39b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "542fed80-23be-5a6a-9fdd-47aa3c2e1b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284298} :answer-field/id #uuid "645d9ca0-1e91-5e12-ac1f-4d79dc2c1b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19cf732b-11c4-5a7a-98ba-06159f5b2c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130455" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $CA=AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93fb6c44-5d1c-548d-b12a-c2d42c762c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 49\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294260} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee1c28-a017-513f-aec8-d20f4a003bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294261} {:answer/id #uuid "0388cfc5-c8d1-5fc5-9831-f028d3cb6239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-49 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294262} {:answer/id #uuid "50b87d23-088b-577f-87fa-71e62dd568a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294263} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff4399e-ed7b-55f3-a569-294cd45859c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 49\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b87d23-088b-577f-87fa-71e62dd568a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294263} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c833db1-4dfe-5b56-8c61-d74f13993f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a1381b5-29b5-5930-b404-a382cf590fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130807" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-8){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77673d0c-9f03-5915-b2ab-4a5d6cb2d94d" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting a Frobenius Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b25f38b-636f-5de0-9797-eccc6742c19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311023} {:answer/id #uuid "84719427-b4e8-59d4-b969-c3e635109c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311024} {:answer/id #uuid "1335d27f-d44b-58a8-b586-dbffda46877b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311025} {:answer/id #uuid "8a80d01f-3d07-50bc-84c7-70282935a7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311026} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4e4587-c1cc-5bcb-b0a6-b223c3c8e9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a80d01f-3d07-50bc-84c7-70282935a7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311026} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c09309c-f061-569e-8c77-bf6cc59031c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7852050f-67a6-578c-976b-548dc012848b" :question/math-academy-id "q-135395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c800bf1-db0e-5a4f-b1f7-79de46c24ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294266} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc1a32-3ddc-5b62-b443-798708784e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294267} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee8162a-22a0-5c9e-b9db-f9b656661d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294269} {:answer/id #uuid "976eda39-25fa-5c0c-ada1-63cab5aa0334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0acda-284b-5a31-8855-d2a8a1ff7df1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2480cdbb-3eea-5c96-bb8f-edcdbea7e886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369079} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6578c2-3e2a-59ca-9395-cf95e3481042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369080} {:answer/id #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369081} {:answer/id #uuid "c30e77af-1243-526a-9dd2-9fac58e17d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369082} {:answer/id #uuid "6adb2792-0256-5852-a7e2-ec880e34fbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{62}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369081} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac69e96d-fec1-590b-97ef-264b252bbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4fc2267c-cf00-5edb-8fcf-dca3892e059b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143876" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6f3aab-e069-585c-a5dd-dcba5897f9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369086} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd95d-e9c3-5cb1-99ca-c48ae9d97099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369087} {:answer/id #uuid "92f65170-b156-5f2e-8b59-42e60774b300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369088} {:answer/id #uuid "e44e619d-6352-5d7a-b131-f45b826bfe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.885" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369089} {:answer/id #uuid "73a703bd-afbc-5e88-956f-88eb56fc44ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.943" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd95d-e9c3-5cb1-99ca-c48ae9d97099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369087} :answer-field/id #uuid "2654228b-08dd-5bec-83b0-6f72a011e1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8c2e20f-0f73-51af-a685-5cac68a35ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148669" :question/problem "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8353b5dc-7cd7-5dd4-8896-24138ab0ad3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247201} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce6c33-30ba-5cb1-83a3-61b4b3b415ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8c732c-3a67-5773-95c5-05e65b555e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17eeee-6e1a-53a8-937d-9232a4cc2c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204} {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cf0431-cc21-5deb-8a40-4019c358ec69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "835d558d-3fba-579b-91a8-f50eb4c92637" :question/math-academy-id "q-15664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7091a30-7cfd-5e7e-a388-650ae636a02e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321947} {:answer/id #uuid "27df7357-7605-5a94-b294-2aff462a1fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321948} {:answer/id #uuid "b533c0af-2dbe-57bd-ac12-4207d4957760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321949} {:answer/id #uuid "0b07e8c3-4c8e-59c5-bfec-14c113f12088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321950} {:answer/id #uuid "77c801ca-c8b5-544c-a29d-14e48f1d2bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b07e8c3-4c8e-59c5-bfec-14c113f12088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321950} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc435495-3a7f-5173-8f2a-5ac136d628f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "534f7159-4e37-511b-a177-3ef2f0afb878" :question/math-academy-id "q-157468" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x+y-2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af1a7f0-21ea-599e-8ae4-8c70b697fdc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294582} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd70fd7-4169-593d-b17d-6524c8aa73d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294583} {:answer/id #uuid "99bea051-55e3-5f06-9ebe-be38839b0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294584} {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} {:answer/id #uuid "cc59692d-8e56-544e-8518-ed211520a029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfa03b0-59e8-5335-aa32-e5c6bb8083d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163548" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} {:answer/id #uuid "59ca6c49-6b6b-5efa-9d96-1f6004f090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{-1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320373} {:answer/id #uuid "f814935a-3005-55a2-aacc-7b2a0929f3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320374} {:answer/id #uuid "58d16a85-9e04-5f05-b7d5-8cf620b3a60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+Cx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320375} {:answer/id #uuid "197b642a-007d-568a-ab5d-e44698445089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} :answer-field/id #uuid "98047d80-b5b5-5e6e-82bb-eb5ebd0f6a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23f7cb90-301b-5baf-815d-3ddcf267e164" :question/math-academy-id "q-17552" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-1+z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-1,\\,Q(x)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x}$ yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-z) & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{-x}z & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x}) & =-{e}^{-x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x})\\,dx\\, & =-\\int {e}^{-x}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91dcc00f-f2c8-5426-abf6-ea1bd962502f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91dcc00f-f2c8-5426-abf6-ea1bd962502f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294280} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc9ed36-2e28-53d3-b481-63ba06aab151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294279} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7b45060-96c6-56b3-8a7a-8c9256323089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7b45060-96c6-56b3-8a7a-8c9256323089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294282} :answer-field/id #uuid "2197653f-5856-51b3-ae3c-3a6c052c22e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813b7692-a886-5db2-af35-a22870db92f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "813b7692-a886-5db2-af35-a22870db92f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294284} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af16cba-3ced-5c9a-b277-4dd0c6700f60" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "926edf84-42b1-556a-b7aa-63fc4baac279" :question/math-academy-id "q-181704" :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n${z}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some cubic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2,$ ${z}_{2}=3-\\text{i},$ and ${z}_{3}=3+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2})(z-{z}_{3}) \\\\  & =(1)(z-2)(z-(3-\\text{i}))(z-(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-z(3+\\text{i})-z(3-\\text{i})+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2)({z}^{2}-6z+10) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-6{z}^{2}+10z-2{z}^{2}+12z-20 \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n{2,3-\\text{i},3+\\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,{z}^{3}-8{z}^{2}+22z-20=0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88c81beb-9b15-56ae-bb46-ce5cbc1d0ea5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using Anticommutativity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3624ea-5bda-5907-aac1-9ee497758727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6j-3k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294287} {:answer/id #uuid "ae133177-1618-5d14-b6e2-296ab61003ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6i-2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294288} {:answer/id #uuid "f18e7b53-2d26-5157-82ee-56b87f8dd998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9j-6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294289} {:answer/id #uuid "f6877b03-36f1-5530-b6f7-2a05f7f903e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9j+2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294290} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5ff3f5-9d6a-5c60-ad94-17325fbe13b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9j+6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18e7b53-2d26-5157-82ee-56b87f8dd998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9j-6k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294289} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74272dc-ecf7-5b7f-8a1c-c37d16d42024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67fdf3c0-7b47-568c-a0de-0f280923b37c" :question/math-academy-id "q-19777" :question/problem "Find $(2a+3c)\\times (b+2c)$, if $a\\times b=3i-2j+k$, $a\\times c=j-2k$ and $b\\times c=2i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a+3c)\\times (b+2c) & = \\\\ ((2a)\\times b)+((2a)\\times (2c))+((3c)\\times b)+((3c)\\times (2c))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2a)\\times b)+((2a)\\times (2c))+((3c)\\times b)+((3c)\\times (2c)) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+(2\\cdot 2)(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+(3\\cdot 2)(c\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+6(c\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $c\\times c=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)+3(c\\times b)+6(c\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c)+6(0) & = \\\\ 2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times b)+4(a\\times c)-3(b\\times c) & = \\\\ 2(3i-2j+k)+4(j-2k)-3(2i-3j) & = \\\\ (6i-4j+2k)+(4j-8k)-(6i-9j) & = \\\\ (6i-6i)+(-4j+4j+9j)+(2k-8k) & = \\\\ 9j-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4ce9fc-ec03-5271-bd17-eb5db2756338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.663" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271602} {:answer/id #uuid "16badea8-1bce-5a97-87ad-61316b81618a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271603} {:answer/id #uuid "fd97beee-79bc-53a8-ae9e-5e4e1f184e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.613" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271604} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa61d5d-c99b-5393-9e1f-c3a7e0f5c9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271605} {:answer/id #uuid "03dcf9bb-0fdc-562e-a51e-3e04be664a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa61d5d-c99b-5393-9e1f-c3a7e0f5c9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7178a590-d365-59b6-abf3-46595991afe4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ba40cf-c229-5faa-87a1-5d86079dac01" :question/math-academy-id "q-26387" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of ${log}_{9},$ what value of $y$ satisfies the equation ${9}^{2y+1}={5}^{-y}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c8ee97e-f2b0-587e-9171-8008f1e0c4aa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e1a8bd-192a-5d8c-911a-e177dfb7ce9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i-2j+4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334720} {:answer/id #uuid "8574fde5-fb29-5ac6-8416-2de97bb2fc76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i-6j+2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334721} {:answer/id #uuid "b11c2512-97aa-5321-8629-5246aea9bddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+2j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334722} {:answer/id #uuid "a7df66a0-c3b2-540a-abf1-e56481e17e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+6j+2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334723} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa5a2b-a015-56c6-ac37-5031c36333b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-6j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa5a2b-a015-56c6-ac37-5031c36333b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-6j-4k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334724} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6eede9-d55d-59ed-8257-0f817d1476ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b190b78e-5c96-53ca-a8be-8f4dce1bb987" :question/math-academy-id "q-32522" :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e1cd94-0168-537b-9253-806228ba636d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "6258d3ee-665e-517c-892c-53b9d2e82a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321193} {:answer/id #uuid "6507e808-0c0b-53ab-83f4-e951283a1a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f460f-a72b-51cf-98a5-9f463a191fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321195} {:answer/id #uuid "b080d755-4f53-5aaa-b708-48dd41959f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f460f-a72b-51cf-98a5-9f463a191fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321195} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec52a582-0b36-573b-9119-43c61aefe9f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cad98f12-8da4-54c8-87ad-6af552687ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556df5ed-b540-58fd-b44a-adb7ddad3eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369107} {:answer/id #uuid "4074a504-6e17-5bd5-80dc-58b7e403bf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369108} {:answer/id #uuid "a74401ea-309f-5294-b052-678f1a681ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369109} {:answer/id #uuid "f558a2c9-9cf1-5bef-90d3-9a12cde26dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369110} {:answer/id #uuid "ef756984-575a-5523-8188-1244943c4e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4074a504-6e17-5bd5-80dc-58b7e403bf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369108} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb18c16-9cae-5b03-a649-2f26b297d661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765540e0-6bf9-5c09-a934-b0d2b992dba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb1e79f-7789-5ee9-8707-8979eaf696ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c91fc5c-380e-5e71-92bf-b258b18e1dfa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386b24bd-3eff-5335-834a-d78cada3240c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336300" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e8a51f-03c5-59c0-b027-d554459af78c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294669} {:answer/id #uuid "712138d7-e620-5be5-b342-8a3843ff1257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294670} {:answer/id #uuid "88282ac6-c4c6-50a3-a829-eed1fc5efb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294671} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f73c9d-7bbc-5af3-8bdc-37f401b89654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294672} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bca0655-c64d-5fdc-9b72-2e0dc772b824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494759ac-e110-5f70-93f7-3b6af7555034" :question/math-academy-id "q-337570" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e865fe4b-b552-5e49-96e0-af34995f5e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335481} {:answer/id #uuid "dd92b623-ea95-5559-b407-a6917af8dcc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335482} {:answer/id #uuid "860d2f15-f8dc-50f2-bc1d-877881de2c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335483} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b377a4-5170-5909-969a-fee5feb2b631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335484} {:answer/id #uuid "13db39f2-ff13-5346-83b5-dc1e2e7c1488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13db39f2-ff13-5346-83b5-dc1e2e7c1488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335485} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35eb022-54e0-5d0a-b4bd-e9bd48e358a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20669a20-777a-5d15-ade0-e739edb8a182" :question/math-academy-id "q-4016" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "616b7aa2-2c55-5a29-a908-545a4bad4376" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e78f7d83-8f03-50a7-98ca-15315cdaa1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369150} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1b613e-f4b3-52f3-bf0c-27b40021a8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369151} {:answer/id #uuid "83a6ed1d-84b6-5db6-8099-6e8d5d474f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369152} {:answer/id #uuid "6393cbad-9a82-5290-abe1-d2c63b65f26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369150} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f0c6b-6959-594d-9567-93b7821e26a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c49a23bb-a7fc-57e9-921a-fc537e2e5220" :question/math-academy-id "q-42567" :question/problem "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0937a2b-c2c9-5851-95f8-98906380fb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357830} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef1e9d3-d0ca-5136-bfc7-7818d2922e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357831} {:answer/id #uuid "3af95393-2575-5229-bc05-c6f36fb0b54d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357832} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce046a4-1ded-5ee3-8d1b-d7366349cab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357833} {:answer/id #uuid "62b29e01-141e-5f84-9c47-b2cfbf282d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ef1e9d3-d0ca-5136-bfc7-7818d2922e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357831} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d9f240-e780-5c74-b003-2aa3f0d7d460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87beaefa-a44a-5f78-b5fb-0779a6230df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-42579" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -12 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5481ebe6-7afc-5959-b9ff-3ac4af78b201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294336} {:answer/id #uuid "30ebfb58-3bc7-569d-95bf-f02e3b8ca8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294337} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7679e4-a83a-5caf-a580-1ef94cc88f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294338} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c9dce6-e0bc-52df-a671-6796ea08e690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294339} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4dd53b-c48e-5dad-815f-a941a41f4766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5481ebe6-7afc-5959-b9ff-3ac4af78b201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294336} :answer-field/id #uuid "506d02c0-5619-5a27-a023-c555e254176f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "549da073-b722-5fcb-9360-923ddd086ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-43780" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $A={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\le 0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1)\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in A$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & \\le 0 \\\\ kf(1) & \\ge k\\cdot 0 \\\\ (kf) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10507d-8411-501b-bf2a-099f1599108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294349} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8890cb1-796d-5e73-9f8e-477954728915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294351} {:answer/id #uuid "f886e3e2-bc69-5e9f-8cd6-9f9f6b4e1c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294352} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f71ba-6aae-578e-ae86-a1f2d811e951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0028443c-1e65-58fd-b318-c517c386e7d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf463b44-c338-5d2b-89e0-a6a466d2f78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294356} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e11bca-7e2b-5108-a443-68916723834e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294357} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0196c7-8e6e-5b19-b105-23b10d835fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294358} {:answer/id #uuid "8e897539-7b0f-5059-85f1-c18f0166caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294359} {:answer/id #uuid "96574573-9d53-57dc-b486-bd82c12e94f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf463b44-c338-5d2b-89e0-a6a466d2f78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294356} :answer-field/id #uuid "e101af72-681a-5404-804d-9c959a6f14f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9625dd0-98eb-5747-8995-333316814407" :question/math-academy-id "q-71876" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $CD=DC={I}_{3}$\n- $CE=EC={I}_{3}$\n- $D={C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $CE=EC={I}_{3}.$ Computing $CE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CE & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf65e50-bb88-5c21-8317-13393f4c132b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=\\frac{3}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f9421d-8284-5c82-bcd4-0dacb6cfc98d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} {:answer/id #uuid "e06fa6db-c8e6-5a00-9d6c-6be9ec3bed60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212} {:answer/id #uuid "8a288eab-e1f2-5e59-9902-802f5d8787ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb9091-9ef3-5320-86d5-25b0773bc71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0710a0c-afe0-53b4-85ec-60d8d04fc9a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9016b9c4-555f-5f54-8e92-6ab14881289d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73243" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t{e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((1-t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-2){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(1-(2)){e}^{-(2)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536de9bd-b925-5fde-9a18-7659e6ac81d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333813} {:answer/id #uuid "0e698b23-e674-562b-9725-672ace33dc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333814} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4df8ba-9dfc-5923-84a3-872dca158f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333815} {:answer/id #uuid "071f479b-04d2-5caa-b345-9f4513703d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333816} {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d78575b-a3e0-576b-b5ff-36e8ed92f2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538bab68-7058-5f4f-b133-c515348f8f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe0d77e-52ec-55af-86ee-7865dc316245" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Monomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae96aae-1f10-5ac7-9d29-105c275549e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273571} {:answer/id #uuid "d8073d17-352d-5ea3-bcfa-4f1cb00b9517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273572} {:answer/id #uuid "d632c816-a222-5e8e-b9ba-45e2d2ef2cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273573} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed832fe-8708-575c-a106-069f39aca48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273574} {:answer/id #uuid "1a59a83f-214f-5cad-98db-156efd569fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(12{w}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae96aae-1f10-5ac7-9d29-105c275549e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{w}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273571} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c3b091d-1720-5e63-b555-a040fcf0b69c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb1d235-c690-5225-8779-e89c17dee989" :question/math-academy-id "q-76311" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $6w,$ $3{w}^{3},$ and $4{w}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three monomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable parts. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,12,18,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,6,9,12,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,8,12,16,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,3,4)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $w,\\,{w}^{3},$ and ${w}^{2},$ we raise $w$ to the largest power that appears in all the expressions, which is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w,{w}^{3},{w}^{2})={w}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6w,3{w}^{3},4{w}^{2})=12\\cdot {w}^{3}=12{w}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d308c4-816e-53bb-9c5e-3a09bfaddfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269145} {:answer/id #uuid "72673e86-c085-5291-8bec-166da72a0e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269146} {:answer/id #uuid "32c95188-10a9-5114-99b0-cb90db5d77b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269147} {:answer/id #uuid "84cd7a4c-ec32-59ce-a7d2-70ca0fd02759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269148} {:answer/id #uuid "d2487dac-0617-58ae-8a51-f5bdcd054680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d308c4-816e-53bb-9c5e-3a09bfaddfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269145} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f26f37a-46b8-5fce-81b3-84be685ab68e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09283c16-ce85-51a9-9e26-ee1e56005470" :question/math-academy-id "q-77159" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(2,4,1),$ $Q(3,4,2)$ and $R(3,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,q=⟨3,4,2⟩,\\,r=⟨3,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨3,3,1⟩-⟨2,4,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed317ee4-e207-50c2-ad9b-2c2bfc3ab6aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294388} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc81b8c-e28b-5f55-9962-a7fef63345d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294389} {:answer/id #uuid "7c725089-0910-595b-8d08-433b4fe7d09c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294390} {:answer/id #uuid "81ed89c9-f756-51c7-889b-63626d2d16e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294391} {:answer/id #uuid "86c44b91-9a74-5cc5-9aaa-75da5bdf6946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bc81b8c-e28b-5f55-9962-a7fef63345d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294389} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2f988c-6068-5c9e-b181-6bd27aa3a81a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77266e3-e110-5646-9023-0f09c4f4c827" :question/math-academy-id "q-77433" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(1,1,0)$ and $B(3,-1,2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,2⟩-⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f783fe8-8f52-5f93-adef-bf4bd030fea0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Vertical Hyperbola in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3937a4c-857f-56da-93a2-afc158d08b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294394} {:answer/id #uuid "10ee06aa-4179-587e-a705-928a9db9a24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294395} {:answer/id #uuid "788849cc-4ada-561e-bb97-b676702087d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294396} {:answer/id #uuid "b4be13f8-f674-5ab7-b854-f5fed230d0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294397} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8d4d70-f021-5f45-a6e8-13c498e94d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4be13f8-f674-5ab7-b854-f5fed230d0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294397} :answer-field/id #uuid "07cffcbd-520f-501a-a932-dd6270e020d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa7228ce-645a-52a8-9a40-e535c79572db" :question/math-academy-id "q-78257" :question/problem "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d03f79a4-06f3-5324-a1ec-687659956384" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations Containing Nonlinear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8a39239-61e0-54d7-9d05-0da895761028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+3,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273736} {:answer/id #uuid "32768fed-e28a-50d5-b9c8-81276fca1f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273737} {:answer/id #uuid "6db5bc71-5084-5b70-8071-509fe66c2611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-1,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273738} {:answer/id #uuid "21dce0ec-c390-5f52-baf7-ed7d3a648b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)+2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273739} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb0e3f4-4801-57b8-a07c-77a7f32648a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-2)-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32768fed-e28a-50d5-b9c8-81276fca1f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-2,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273737} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac838a6-f659-5dae-ad35-b0552d1a7f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28eb4849-33b4-5a68-b600-726a71471105" :question/math-academy-id "q-79870" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}(-3x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(8) & ={log}_{4}(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}(5) & ={log}_{4}(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a46a3bc-9089-599a-a63f-9fc68a7eeeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369177} {:answer/id #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369178} {:answer/id #uuid "e312aa09-d16e-5084-a55f-f0d28e042abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369179} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8d3569-d110-5d71-8155-d4acf556257c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369180} {:answer/id #uuid "1e895897-5109-50c8-9869-355a5ce0aea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369178} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b31f61a-2eb7-5caa-8974-71cfeeb1b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229858c7-5c8f-5030-8583-21587b3271c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80778" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98744a46-1ddc-5d16-939d-fc52da67c11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283866} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0cf815-df8d-5406-801e-49e27bdf5726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283867} {:answer/id #uuid "a816077d-0b79-5326-b761-d4032a5bdc86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283868} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e6c023-fced-5e51-9290-8d99c38b7209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283869} {:answer/id #uuid "27b17022-e430-5321-a4f4-dcc39497578a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98744a46-1ddc-5d16-939d-fc52da67c11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283866} :answer-field/id #uuid "da278268-0953-57c8-8371-23abd5230202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf0d1bd1-6767-5355-bf10-b6a1baf057d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-85172" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(10,5),(0,-5),$ and $(15,20).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(9,5)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(10,-7)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{35-30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 15 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 50 & -125 \\\\ 5 & -35 & 95\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & -25 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 19\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(0,1),(10,-7),(-25,19).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a55a708-abb0-5e23-8862-153561d022c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complement of a Set Without a Venn Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c394f980-ab99-540e-bbbf-6ee9e03438a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4,8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294415} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5c09b4-4d46-5559-b9ec-ec713538fe22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294416} {:answer/id #uuid "722c1b51-99fc-5a0f-9c45-026ba6f2e0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2,4,8,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294417} {:answer/id #uuid "064cf5f7-3c6f-5a67-8ace-d0b029af5d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4,8,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294418} {:answer/id #uuid "d6964215-98b3-59b2-9c5f-b58ada618e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4,8,10,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "064cf5f7-3c6f-5a67-8ace-d0b029af5d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{4,8,12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294418} :answer-field/id #uuid "9849dc9d-cdf7-5cd6-9b8f-d1714b2f7f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0c292a3-44f7-5419-b7a7-a7561f0dc7c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86590" :question/problem "If $U={2,4,6,8,10,12}$ is the universal set and $B={2,6,10},$ then the complement of $B$ is" :question/worked-solution "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $4,$ $8,$   and $12.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{B}={4,8,12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eab4249-544e-56c6-81c0-e6db84033701" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance Given the Number, Sum, and Sum of Squares of the Data Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79aa2e40-f4d2-5709-8b5b-54790b86acf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261940} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dbac9f-85f4-59c4-8a53-10bde3633c1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dfa7943-f9fd-587a-96e7-82a3ae160d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-87562" :question/problem "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $9$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c63c6329-411c-5ee5-a6fb-c199983b9670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294823} {:answer/id #uuid "84fccc02-cfba-59ba-965b-cf1df9b5fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294824} {:answer/id #uuid "517f550c-47fc-55d5-aaff-7f19a5e4f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294825} {:answer/id #uuid "69f32dbb-5860-546d-951c-b71903cf942b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294826} {:answer/id #uuid "772c5e5c-a173-5bff-9c9f-6280a74d301e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f32dbb-5860-546d-951c-b71903cf942b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294826} :answer-field/id #uuid "482c157c-a744-5559-9cdf-215aac443a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a38e97ba-c156-5496-ad06-15f48100971c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89885" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb496a6-bdd6-5c5d-acc9-7c29122660b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342406} {:answer/id #uuid "a589d169-a1ee-5150-ac2c-095f321aba93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342407} {:answer/id #uuid "771e051c-6b45-5bc6-b7d2-e86074f82c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342408} {:answer/id #uuid "d0579c9e-54e5-5b1e-af23-52ef4a525a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342409} {:answer/id #uuid "07e0c7b7-fbee-5b27-bcbf-4a24349c7fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0579c9e-54e5-5b1e-af23-52ef4a525a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342409} :answer-field/id #uuid "a154e459-ecf9-5d97-be47-1d6d77cb2c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9079775-d7f3-54f6-b251-e9776a901ea7" :question/math-academy-id "q-9253" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1979b91d-2ac0-5d35-b4cc-1c9c9505b503" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tanh Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} {:answer/id #uuid "1be660e5-4491-5cc2-a883-603f1c398d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294845} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0eb8f8-92ae-5019-9d5c-faa2978588e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294846} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c5272-40a5-58cd-912a-33a4cd05f646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294847} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f14128-a178-5bb3-afd9-439f540096b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1a91e0-b7d1-5713-8e41-61b2622139c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bc13545-d6a0-5612-9a84-04aec6ad0b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95505" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "273bab01-0930-5580-b073-8bafe7505887" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Square of the Hyperbolic Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d3b2b-1ff8-5162-9803-5cdc14fde36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327983} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae7dbf8-9d35-59f4-8fb4-f5967daa274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaf322b-af0a-5832-9ab1-5005664b314b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327985} {:answer/id #uuid "ac80e5c1-9497-5654-9017-aebc7b0be494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327986} {:answer/id #uuid "6adc60b9-17a8-5f75-a61a-a023629cf8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{coth}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae7dbf8-9d35-59f4-8fb4-f5967daa274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d77f60d-e9ec-5547-bf6b-a7c070b2c1dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59346a59-f274-5f87-97f7-8c14ba6c65f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96055" :question/problem "$\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)=-{csch}^{2}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbbf05b-8162-539c-8113-e859571ef9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369219} {:answer/id #uuid "96e98df2-7173-5032-9187-e75a8fcc7098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369220} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff6750e-5b03-5487-82ba-4c0f1e9b42e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369221} {:answer/id #uuid "e74c42b7-d4a4-5ebd-ae89-0b7846a93317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369222} {:answer/id #uuid "9953192b-24ce-5a1e-8f27-04b379848220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e74c42b7-d4a4-5ebd-ae89-0b7846a93317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369222} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3ab181-a050-5268-94ed-94744cae622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "519b7e3f-5e6b-5321-a66f-0a3fdc3bd943" :question/math-academy-id "q-98525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)"}]}
